A||N||2||Haze_Performance||'88 Front wheel offset question.||||14||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||04-30-2001||05:01 PM||||Im looking at a 16"x7" wheel for my '88 Formula. I dont want the wheel/tire to stick out at all on the front. With a 7" wide wheel, what offset should I use to keep it flush looking?<P>Thanks<BR><I>Haze</I><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">    </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||14
Z||000001||Wannabe||04-30-2001||06:08 PM||||48 mm offset with a 7" wheel width will keep it flush. The problem is that the back has to stay with the factory offset if you are going 7" in back too. You can probably get away with 42mm and have the front wheels stick out a bit, and use 7mm spacers in back so that you can use the same wheels all around. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||04-30-2001||06:47 PM||||Hmmmmmm. 48mm in front huh?? I was going to use 35mm in the rear. What do ya think?<P>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">    </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||12
